insert into public.drafts(id,slug,content,version,updated_at,published_at) values ('11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111','customers-who-never-ordered','{"id":"11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111","slug":"customers-who-never-ordered","title":"Customers who never ordered","summary":"Find the missing relationship. A small join problem with a useful lesson about NULL.","difficulty":"Easy","tags":["Joins","Subqueries"],"isSample":true,"problem":"<p>A shop stores its customers and their orders in two tables. Return the names of customers who have never placed an order, sorted by customer ID.</p><p>A customer can place several orders. Every order belongs to exactly one customer.</p>","approach":"<p>Start with every customer, then attach any matching orders. A LEFT JOIN keeps customers even when no order matches. The unmatched rows are the ones we need.</p>","sql":"SELECT c.name AS customer\nFROM customers AS c\nLEFT JOIN orders AS o\n    ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.id IS NULL\nORDER BY c.id;","explanation":"<ol><li>The LEFT JOIN preserves all rows from customers.</li><li>For Mira and Dev, no matching order exists, so the order columns are NULL.</li><li>Filtering on o.id IS NULL returns those unmatched customers. Check the order''s non-null primary key rather than a nullable business column.</li><li>ORDER BY c.id makes the result deterministic.</li></ol>","takeaways":"<p>Use an anti-join to find missing relationships. Use IS NULL rather than = NULL, because NULL represents an unknown value.</p>","alternative":"SELECT c.name AS customer\nFROM customers AS c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o\n    WHERE o.customer_id = c.id\n)\nORDER BY c.id;","performance":"<p>An index on orders(customer_id) can help the join or correlated lookup. Compare plans with EXPLAIN on your real data; neither formulation is universally faster.</p>","before":[{"id":"s1","name":"customers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[["1","Asha"],["2","Mira"],["3","Rohan"],["4","Dev"]]},{"id":"s2","name":"orders","columns":["id","customer_id"],"rows":[["101","1"],["102","3"],["103","1"]]}],"after":[{"id":"s3","name":"result","columns":["customer"],"rows":[["Mira"],["Dev"]]}]}'::jsonb,1,'2026-01-03T10:00:00Z','2026-01-03T10:00:00Z') on conflict do nothing;
