insert into public.drafts(id,slug,content,version,updated_at,published_at) values ('33333333-3333-4333-8333-333333333333','three-day-login-streaks','{"id":"33333333-3333-4333-8333-333333333333","slug":"three-day-login-streaks","title":"Finding three-day login streaks","summary":"Turn scattered login dates into consecutive streaks using gaps and islands.","difficulty":"Hard","tags":["Window functions","CTEs","Date functions"],"isSample":true,"problem":"<p>Find every consecutive login streak lasting at least three days for each user. Return user ID, streak start, streak end, and number of days. A user may log in more than once on a date; count each date once.</p><p>login_date is a DATE column. Use MySQL 8.0 or later.</p>","approach":"<p>Remove duplicate user-date pairs first. Number each user''s remaining dates chronologically. Subtract that row number from each date: dates in a consecutive run will produce the same anchor date. Group those anchors to form streaks.</p>","sql":"WITH distinct_days AS (\n    SELECT DISTINCT user_id, login_date\n    FROM logins\n), numbered AS (\n    SELECT user_id, login_date,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n               PARTITION BY user_id\n               ORDER BY login_date\n           ) AS rn\n    FROM distinct_days\n), islands AS (\n    SELECT user_id, login_date,\n           DATE_SUB(login_date, INTERVAL rn DAY) AS anchor\n    FROM numbered\n)\nSELECT user_id, MIN(login_date) AS streak_start,\n       MAX(login_date) AS streak_end, COUNT(*) AS days\nFROM islands\nGROUP BY user_id, anchor\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 3\nORDER BY user_id, streak_start;","explanation":"<p>For user 1, January 1, 2, and 3 receive row numbers 1, 2, and 3. Subtracting those numbers gives December 31 each time. January 5 and 6 receive row numbers 4 and 5 and share a different anchor, January 1.</p><p>Grouping by user and anchor separates the runs. COUNT measures the streak length, while MIN and MAX find its boundaries. HAVING keeps runs of at least three days.</p><p>Deduplication is essential: otherwise multiple logins on a date change the row numbers and break the grouping.</p>","takeaways":"<p>Gaps and islands problems often become simpler after deriving a stable group key. Always define the grain of your input before applying a window function.</p>","alternative":"","performance":"<p>A composite index on (user_id, login_date) supports the deduplication and ordering keys. Inspect the plan on realistic volumes because CTE materialization and sorting costs depend on the MySQL optimizer.</p>","before":[{"id":"s6","name":"logins","columns":["user_id","login_date"],"rows":[["1","2026-01-01"],["1","2026-01-02"],["1","2026-01-02"],["1","2026-01-03"],["1","2026-01-05"],["1","2026-01-06"],["2","2026-01-08"],["2","2026-01-09"],["2","2026-01-10"]]}],"after":[{"id":"s7","name":"result","columns":["user_id","streak_start","streak_end","days"],"rows":[["1","2026-01-01","2026-01-03","3"],["2","2026-01-08","2026-01-10","3"]]}]}'::jsonb,1,'2026-01-01T10:00:00Z','2026-01-01T10:00:00Z') on conflict do nothing;
